  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "lesson-1",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-1",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Intro to Loops",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Intro to Loops",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do while loops decide how long we repeat?**\n\nThis lesson introduces the concept of iteration (loops) through real-world scenarios starting with the \"Paper Toss Challenge,\" where they experience the difference between repeating an action a fixed number of times versus repeating until a specific condition is met. Then, students create flowcharts to model a for loop and while loop. Finally, students utilize AI to translate their  flowcharts into text-based pseudocode.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Provide students with a copy of the Flowcharting Loops Activity Guide",
        "student_overview": "**How do while loops decide how long we repeat?**\n\nLearn about the concept of iteration loops through real-world scenarios, as well as visual and text-based representations."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Intro to Loops",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Counted Repetition",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Counted Repetition",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do for loops control how many times we repeat?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how for loops control repeated actions by acting out loop behavior, debugging a broken password generator, and creating a simple animation using AI-assisted code. They learn how start, stop, and step values affect loop outcomes and how small logic mistakes lead to off-by-one errors. By the end, students can analyze loop behavior, correct boundary issues, and use AI intentionally to generate and refine functional loop-based code.",
        "student_overview": "**How do for loops control how many times we repeat?**\n\nToday you learn how for loops make apps repeat actions and how a small mistake in a loop can cause a program to run the wrong number of times. You act out a loop, fix a bug in a real app, and build a mini animation with help from AI. By the end, you understand how loop settings like start, stop, and step control what your program does."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Counted Repetition",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Open-Ended Repetition",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Open-Ended Repetition",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do while loops decide how long we repeat?**\n\nThis lesson introduces students to open-ended repetition using `while` loops, distinguishing them from the fixed repetition of `for` loops. Students explore how `while` loops rely on a condition being true to continue running and false to stop. They use flowcharts and debugging exercises to identify and fix \"infinite loops\" which prepares them to design a mini dice roller game.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Provide students with a copy of the Debug a While Loop slide deck",
        "student_overview": "**How do while loops decide how long we repeat?**\n\nExplore open-ended repetition using `while` loops, by debugging flowcharts and incorrect code before building your own mini-game."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Open-Ended Repetition",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Displaying Arrays as List Views",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Displaying Arrays as List Views",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do loops traverse arrays to render a basic view?**\n\nIn this lesson, students learn how apps use loops to filter data and present clear, trustworthy list views by adding transparency cues such as counts, filter summaries, and empty states. They then build a full read → transform → render pipeline that intentionally controls what information is shown and how it appears. By the end, students can design list views that communicate system behavior clearly and use looping logic to produce purposeful, user-centered displays.",
        "student_overview": "**How do loops traverse arrays to render a basic view?**\n\nToday you learn how apps decide what to show by using loops to filter data and display results in a clear, helpful way. You improve a list view by adding messages that explain what’s happening and then build your own pipeline that reads data, filters it, and shows only what users need. By the end, you understand how your app makes display decisions and how to make those decisions feel clear and trustworthy."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Displaying Arrays as List Views",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Displaying Objects as Cards",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Displaying Objects as Cards",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we render richer records reliably?**\n\nStudents learn how to turn objects from a dataset into clear, readable cards by choosing which fields to show and adding simple fallbacks when information is missing. They practice sketching a card layout, coding a loop to display multiple cards, and noticing how design choices can shape how users interpret data. The lesson focuses on helping students present information in a way that is organized, honest, and easy to understand.",
        "preparation": "- (Optional) Provide students with a copy of the Diagram a Data Schema activity guide.",
        "student_overview": "**How do we render richer records reliably?**\n\nLearn how to turn data objects into clear, readable cards by choosing key fields, handling missing information, and recognizing how design choices shape what users see."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Displaying Objects as Cards",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Filtering and Transparency",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Filtering and Transparency",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can a filter prevent or reinforce biases?**\n\nIn this lesson, students examine how hidden filter defaults in apps can unintentionally exclude users and then apply that insight by coding a transparent, Boolean-based filter in a Volunteer Finder tool. They use AND logic to combine multiple conditions and update the interface so users can clearly see why certain results appear. By the end, students understand how filtering choices shape user experience and how transparency helps prevent misleading or exclusionary outcomes.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Filtering and Transparency activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can a filter prevent or reinforce biases?**\n\nToday you explore how apps sometimes hide important filter rules and how that can limit what people see. You then build your own filter for a Volunteer Finder tool using AND logic to control which roles appear and add clear messages so users know why they’re seeing those results. By the end, you understand how your filtering decisions affect who gets included and how to make those decisions more fair and transparent."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Filtering and Transparency",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Pure Functions vs Side Effects",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Pure Functions vs Side Effects",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What’s the difference between transforming data and causing effects?**\n\nThis lesson introduces students to the difference between functions that safely return new data and ones that accidentally change the original. Through a hands-on sticky-note activity and simple method investigations, students see how mutation happens and why copying data matters. They finish by using AI to help fix a buggy function, practicing how to check whether code is truly safe.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Pure Functions vs Side Effects activity guide (one for each student)\n- Gather a small stack of sticky notes for each student",
        "student_overview": "**What’s the difference between transforming data and causing effects?**\n\nLearn how to spot which functions safely create new data and which ones accidentally change the original, and use that understanding to fix buggy code with AI support."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pure Functions vs Side Effects",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Copy, Don't Mutate",
      "name": "Lesson 8: Copy, Don't Mutate",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**When should we copy data, and how do users recover data that has been modified?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore why apps need reliable Reset and Undo features and how those features depend on copying data rather than mutating it. They design a user journey showing how these actions should work, then fix a broken Reset function and build an Undo history stack using state snapshots and push/pop logic. By the end, students understand how the copy-don’t-mutate strategy preserves data integrity and enables predictable recovery behaviors in interactive systems.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Copy, Don’t Mutate activity guide (one for each student)\n- Gather a small stack of sticky notes for each student",
        "student_overview": "**When should we copy data, and how do users recover data that has been modified?**\n\nToday you learn how apps let users recover from mistakes by using safe copies of data instead of changing the original. You design how Reset and Undo should work for a real interface, fix a broken Reset button, and build an Undo feature that steps back through saved history states. By the end, you see how keeping clean copies of data helps your app stay reliable and lets users reverse actions without losing information."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Copy, Don't Mutate",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**Who is surfaced or sidelined by our filters, and how can we show that?**\n\nIn this lesson, students investigate how categories and thresholds quietly shape visibility in apps and how certain voices or items disappear as filters become more restrictive. Through hands-on exploration, they examine who gets excluded and why transparency matters in user-facing tools. Students then redesign filters and labels to prioritize inclusion and visibility and include explanations to help users understand why results appear the way they do. Students see how filtering choices influence fairness and trust, and how thoughtful design prevents misleading or exclusionary outcomes.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**Who is surfaced or sidelined by our filters, and how can we show that?**\n\nExplore how filters can unintentionally hide people or items. Learn how to redesign them so users understand what they’re seeing and why."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Inclusive Filters and Clear Labels",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Schema Design for Simples Features",
      "name": "Lesson 10: Schema Design for Simple Features",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How should our data shape make features simpler, correct, and fair?**\n\nIn this lesson, students investigate how inconsistent field names and data types lead to misinterpretation by both humans and AI in real-world datasets. They refactor a flawed schema, then write a loop that cleans messy records by standardizing keys and converting values into reliable types. By the end, students understand why strict schemas are essential for accurate reasoning and how automated cleaning ensures data can be safely processed by downstream features.",
        "student_overview": "**How should our data shape make features simpler, correct, and fair?**\n\nToday you’ll see how unclear or inconsistent data can confuse an app or AI, even when everything looks fine at first glance. You’ll fix a messy dataset by renaming fields, cleaning values, and writing a loop that transforms each record into a consistent format. By the end, you’ll understand why apps need clean, predictable data and how your code helps them interpret information correctly."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Schema Design for Simples Features",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Interactive Data Dashboard",
      "name": "Lesson 11: Interactive Data Dashboard Project",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we design an interactive dashboard that helps people find what they need while keeping algorithmic choices fair, transparent, and under user control?**\n\nThis project guides students through building a data dashboard that renders items dynamically and supports interactive filtering, sorting, and Reset/Undo behavior. Along the way, students analyze the ethical implications of their filtering logic, refine the UI for clarity and usability, and document their iterative debugging process. AI functions as a partner in reasoning and refinement, with students explaining how AI suggestions shaped their final designs.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Interactive Data Dashboard Project Planning Guide to students (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we design an interactive dashboard that helps people find what they need while keeping algorithmic choices fair, transparent, and under user control?**\n\nIn this project, you build an interactive data dashboard that displays information clearly and lets users explore it with filters, sorting tools, and a Reset/Undo feature. You also examine how your design choices affect fairness and visibility, and use AI to help plan, debug, and refine your work."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Interactive Data Dashboard",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-4",
        "script.name": "iterating-with-ai-pilot-2025"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-2",
      "name": "End of Unit Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 13,
