  "activity_sections": [
    {
      "key": "00dbeee4-e068-4970-94fd-a7d7f993540a",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Pre-Assessment"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "00dbeee4-e068-4970-94fd-a7d7f993540a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "de6147df-40f4-4956-b71b-58ff0ef85e4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e08da8f2-5a60-4d7d-975e-cbebfe610e96",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e08da8f2-5a60-4d7d-975e-cbebfe610e96",
        "lesson_activity.key": "de6147df-40f4-4956-b71b-58ff0ef85e4f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "99bb5da3-fb6f-41f1-a9d4-89c20f4fca9f",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate students' intuition about how different kinds of repetition can take more or less time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Which round do you think would take longer—and why?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface the idea that a fixed number of repetitions (like five tosses) is different from repeating until a condition is met (making a basket), and that the condition-based round can take more or fewer tosses depending on what happens.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students might have different answers for which round would take longer depending on their skill level in basketball. The important takeaway is that Round 1 will always involve 5 free throws and Round 2 will depend on how long it takes to sink a free throw."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "99bb5da3-fb6f-41f1-a9d4-89c20f4fca9f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "94675dfc-d0c8-4715-a1c2-b4c18ae3082e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9685c4d0-3802-4bea-960e-336c318b292d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9685c4d0-3802-4bea-960e-336c318b292d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "94675dfc-d0c8-4715-a1c2-b4c18ae3082e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cba85ce9-abd5-4b33-bb5d-9dec81e844ce",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cba85ce9-abd5-4b33-bb5d-9dec81e844ce",
        "lesson_activity.key": "94675dfc-d0c8-4715-a1c2-b4c18ae3082e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "956c43d8-f3ea-42dd-80e4-dd1498b51537",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Allow students to experience two different types of loops in a physical challenge.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Make sure each student has one sheet of paper to crumple into a ball and that there is at least one wastebasket or clear target area available for each group.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If space is limited, have students take turns in small groups or choose one or two students to volunteer in front of the class."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "956c43d8-f3ea-42dd-80e4-dd1498b51537",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d91a2b38-2ffb-4ead-af26-952b28aca5b1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d91a2b38-2ffb-4ead-af26-952b28aca5b1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "375be062-3e48-4e6a-8f0e-6a1bb84f6ac6",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students repeat the toss exactly five times, whether they make or miss the basket. Have them record their W/L sequence for all five tosses.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students stop after exactly five tosses, and that they record a clear sequence of W and L.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Select one or two students to write their W/L sequence on the board to compare them."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "375be062-3e48-4e6a-8f0e-6a1bb84f6ac6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "195b9784-b31f-487d-894b-07af7eb19ace",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "195b9784-b31f-487d-894b-07af7eb19ace",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bd49a347-e7e9-473a-a1bf-714b4e5de696",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students keep tossing until they make basket. Once they make it, have them note how many tosses it took.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Watch that students stop right after their first W. Ask questions like: How did you know when to stop? Did everyone need the same number of tosses? Help students notice that the number of repetitions varies from person to person.\n",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If a student is struggling to make a basket after many tries, set a safety stop (for example, ten tries) and have them record that the loop would stop for safety in a real program."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bd49a347-e7e9-473a-a1bf-714b4e5de696",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb1667e1-5b4a-4f70-9423-26e0184461b9",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb1667e1-5b4a-4f70-9423-26e0184461b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6e6ced23-e17e-4bed-bf44-28fc0d3b475f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *In each round, how did you know when to stop?*\n- *Which round took longer for you?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students articulate that in the first round, they stopped after a fixed count. In the second round, the stopped when a condition became true. The second round was shorter or longer depending on how long it took them to reach the true condition (make a basket).\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students distinguish between loops that repeat a set number of times and loops that repeat until a condition changes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that both types are examples of iteration, but they are controlled differently. Give a quick verbal example of each, such as doing ten jumping jacks (count-controlled) versus reciting a poem until you memorize it (condition-controlled).",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Ask students to think of everyday examples as either “count-controlled” or “condition-controlled” to prime them for creating for loop and while loop flowcharts."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6e6ced23-e17e-4bed-bf44-28fc0d3b475f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "54f7074b-d95f-4ef9-a8f5-6c663f7ba5da",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *iteration*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 2.\n- Level 1: Read the definition and laundry example of iteration.\n- Level 2: Write your own definition of iteration and include a real-world example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check students' Level 2 responses for a clear sense of \"repeating a set of steps,\" not just \"repeating something.\" Ask probing questions like \"What steps get repeated? How many times?\"",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Iteration",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 2 responses to gauge who has a solid grasp on iteration before moving on. Note any students who need more concrete examples so you can support them in later levels."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "54f7074b-d95f-4ef9-a8f5-6c663f7ba5da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "330b250b-aaa9-4d4e-8a2c-96d3f06b28f7",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "330b250b-aaa9-4d4e-8a2c-96d3f06b28f7",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f8317302-dac7-49c1-8889-92cb478c3c15",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate students' prior knowledge about repeating steps and noticing when they stop.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the ideas of start, stop, and step as the three pieces that control how many times a for loop runs. Connect them back to an everyday example. For example, going up stairs you start at the bottom (start value = step 0), stop at the top (stop value = step 6), and go up one step at a time (step = 1). Different staircases might require different start, stop, or step values.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What are some examples of tasks you repeat, and how do you know when to stop?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Surface that many real-world tasks involve repeating the same steps, and that there is usually some signal or condition that tells us when we are finished",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to use the new word \"iteration\" in describing their example by identifying what counts as one full iteration and estimating how many iterations they usually do."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f8317302-dac7-49c1-8889-92cb478c3c15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "829dc2c9-af2e-466a-999e-f5d3c0a5ea28",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "829dc2c9-af2e-466a-999e-f5d3c0a5ea28",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0f735fb9-486c-45a3-9bfd-ce584db8cf58",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 5. Tell them they will explore a model for loop flowchart, then design their own representing something meaningful to them. Optional - Direct students to complete the Flowcharting Loops activity guide on paper and sketch out their flowchart by hand. Remind students to pick a task with a known number of repeats (for example, ten shots, three sets of an exercise, five questions). Emphasize labeling start, stop, and step.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students' flowcharts include an initialization (start value), a clear stopping point, and a step that changes the counter. Ask questions like \"Where does your loop start?\" \"How do you know when it stops?\" and \"What changes each time?\" Redirect any tasks that do not have a fixed number of repetitions.",
        "progression_name": "For Loop Flowchart"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0f735fb9-486c-45a3-9bfd-ce584db8cf58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "18142f51-2b13-4643-aee9-46fccdaf9fac",
      "position": 13,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: For Loop",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "For students who are stuck choosing a scenario, offer the prompt list from the level (video games, baking cookies, practicing free throws, going to class) and help them pick one they know well."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "18142f51-2b13-4643-aee9-46fccdaf9fac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5b5bb340-70cf-4c00-bd70-d7cb196817fc",
      "position": 14,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5b5bb340-70cf-4c00-bd70-d7cb196817fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5ce36921-6916-4afe-89f9-9cfd2530d0a2",
      "position": 15,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *for loop*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that in condition-controlled loops, the number of repetitions is not fixed ahead of time. The loop continues as long as some condition is true, such as \"while light is green\" or \"while light is red.\"",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Contrast quickly with their for loop flowcharts: ask, \"Could you say exactly how many times the loop runs in this example? Why or why not?\" to highlight the difference."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5ce36921-6916-4afe-89f9-9cfd2530d0a2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de1408a6-b215-4cc7-a3d9-19f53f181c06",
      "position": 16,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de1408a6-b215-4cc7-a3d9-19f53f181c06",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0b27a3b8-398e-4c85-b94f-4a28461ced65",
      "position": 17,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 6 to complete Levels 6 through 8. Explain that they will explore a model while loop flowchart and then create one of their own where repetition depends on a condition. Emphasize choosing a situation where you do not know the exact number of repeats ahead of time. For example, studying until you feel ready, checking messages until inbox is empty, playing until someone wins.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for a clear condition in the decision diamond and a path that updates something so the loop can eventually end. Ask, \"What has to change for this loop to stop?\" and \"Where in your flowchart does that change happen?\" Help students avoid infinite loops where the condition never becomes false.",
        "progression_name": "While Loop Flowchart"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0b27a3b8-398e-4c85-b94f-4a28461ced65",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eb8767cc-0da8-42c1-8839-4370a7f84346",
      "position": 18,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: While Loop",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students confuse for and while, invite them to take their earlier for loop task and reframe it as a while loop example by focusing on a condition instead of a fixed count."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eb8767cc-0da8-42c1-8839-4370a7f84346",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fe724f9a-bd67-479b-8d38-b8de77de384a",
      "position": 19,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fe724f9a-bd67-479b-8d38-b8de77de384a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f516f052-940a-40e3-8100-7c8e484ab3cc",
      "position": 20,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *while loop*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *When would you use a for loop? When would you use a while loop?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students articulate that you would use a for loop for a known count with a clear stop value and step value. And you would use a while loop for evaluating a condition.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Ask students to quickly jot down one real-world example for each type of loop, labeling them clearly as for or while. Collect a few examples to share out if time allows. Use their responses to see if they can correctly match scenarios to count-controlled or condition-controlled loops; note any confusion to revisit in the next lesson."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f516f052-940a-40e3-8100-7c8e484ab3cc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0565d26d-10a1-4719-924e-f1d1f97fc030",
      "position": 21,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0565d26d-10a1-4719-924e-f1d1f97fc030",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3b6969a-af3b-42ad-8ec8-bdb7df5374b9",
      "position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 9. Explain that they will upload a screenshot of one of their flowcharts to AI to translate it into pseudocode. Have students rewrite their flowcharts in plain language (pseudocode). Emphasize that getting the logic right matters more than exact coding syntax right now. Encourage students to edit the AI's suggestion so it accurately matches their diagrams.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are uploading an image of their flowchart to AI and reading over the resulting pseudocode. Ask questions like \"Where is your start value here?\" \"How do you know when this loop stops?\" Support students in correcting AI output that is inaccurate or incomplete.",
        "progression_name": "Loops in Pseudocode",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Reinforce responsible AI use: AI can suggest pseudocode, but students are the ones who must check for accuracy and safety. Prompt them to explain any AI-generated content in their own words."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3b6969a-af3b-42ad-8ec8-bdb7df5374b9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "aa61a80c-8306-4241-9b94-933421f4c1d7"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "70c82acf-881d-4cb3-8791-9b5e68834269",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review each vocabulary term."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "70c82acf-881d-4cb3-8791-9b5e68834269",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d3e2546b-2301-45ae-8399-6f19432c3595"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c32b13e4-8dde-40ea-a885-33f45974c721",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 10 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c32b13e4-8dde-40ea-a885-33f45974c721",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d3e2546b-2301-45ae-8399-6f19432c3595"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "10936cb4-028c-481f-93df-77c52fdf56c8",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students physically experience how a for-loop condition controls how many times a loop runs.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have the class count in unison. After they say 4, pause and emphasize that the loop stops before 5 because the condition is i < 5.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>**Circulate:** Watch for students who keep going past 4. Ask them what the condition i < 5 means and whether 5 is less than 5. Help them connect the math comparison to stopping the loop.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students count again from 0 and let them continue until they say 5. Pause and draw attention to the fact that they said one extra number compared to the first run.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for who stops at 4 versus who keeps going. Ask students to compare i < 5 and i <= 5.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How many numbers did we say in Round 1?*\n- *How many numbers did we say in Round 2?*\n- *What changed in the code?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students notice that changing < to <= adds exactly one more count, and that a single symbol can change the number of loop repetitions by one. Use this to foreshadow that this kind of mistake is called an off-by-one error.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "10936cb4-028c-481f-93df-77c52fdf56c8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "20389b9f-e23c-45c2-b7e2-fc5a72d00f51"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "faceb37a-8397-4df7-9507-a139f153312c",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *loop variable*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *start value*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *stop value*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *step value*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 2.\n- Level 1: Read the definition and visual for \"loop variable.\"\n- Level 2: Explain \"loop variable\" in their own words with an example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen to how students describe the loop variable. Prompt them to connect it to the counting they did in the warm up and to i in a for loop. Ask, \"What does this variable keep track of?\" and \"How does changing it affect the loop?\"",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Loop Variable",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look for students who can clearly state that the loop variable is what changes each time the loop runs and that it controls how many times the loop repeats."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "faceb37a-8397-4df7-9507-a139f153312c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f926b7d3-a5ab-473b-8fbe-b8eaba586a9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85db97b4-a738-4a2d-b73b-8b441eba1e77",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85db97b4-a738-4a2d-b73b-8b441eba1e77",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f926b7d3-a5ab-473b-8fbe-b8eaba586a9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "82ee7703-bcce-4ce5-a44a-a9f09c18b711",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce Quality Assurance (QA) teams."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "82ee7703-bcce-4ce5-a44a-a9f09c18b711",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f926b7d3-a5ab-473b-8fbe-b8eaba586a9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "6d150c85-8d35-471e-a14e-9739930b66e9",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "For this activity, you are the Quality Assurance, or QA, team. A QA team's job is to thoroughly test an application to find any unexpected bugs. Your task is to test this password generator by running it and checking to see if the password length always matches the number you requested.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "6d150c85-8d35-471e-a14e-9739930b66e9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f926b7d3-a5ab-473b-8fbe-b8eaba586a9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "145ea16f-01cd-4367-adb8-a4c6f5803818",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 2, Level 3 to complete Levels 3 through 5. Frame students as the QA (Quality Assurance) team testing a password generator. Ask them to run the code multiple times with different requested lengths and pay attention to how many characters they actually get.\n- Level 3: Debug the Broken Password Generator so that the generated password has the same number of characters entered.\n- Level 4: Read the definition and visual for \"off-by-one error.\"\n- Level 5: Explain \"off-by-one error\" in their own words with an example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Encourage students to look specifically at the loop that builds the password. Ask, \"What is the loop variable here?\" and \"How many times does this loop run compared to the number the user asks for?\" Support them in trying small changes like adjusting < vs <= or the loop value start value (e.g. let i = 0;), and testing after each change.",
        "progression_name": "Broken Password Generator"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "145ea16f-01cd-4367-adb8-a4c6f5803818",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f926b7d3-a5ab-473b-8fbe-b8eaba586a9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "917f53fd-e360-4949-987f-d106a9a530d8",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Off-By-One Error",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students are stuck, suggest they display the loop variable's value directly in a visible text label or on the page each time the loop runs so they can visually track the loop's steps and see how many iterations are happening. They can also trace the code on paper if needed."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Check that students can correctly describe an off-by-one error as a loop that runs one extra or one fewer time than intended, and can connect it to a concrete example."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "917f53fd-e360-4949-987f-d106a9a530d8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f926b7d3-a5ab-473b-8fbe-b8eaba586a9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1ee2bc8e-8b33-4ad6-8a0d-bc938d771ea6",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1ee2bc8e-8b33-4ad6-8a0d-bc938d771ea6",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f926b7d3-a5ab-473b-8fbe-b8eaba586a9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d04a7767-1570-47d6-967b-c713dec69db3",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *off-by-one error*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animated slide demonstrate the exact mechanics of a for-loop and identify an off-by-one error when the count runs one time too many.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *To prevent off-by-one errors, why is testing the loop's start value, last value, and the stop condition more critical than testing a number in the middle of the loop's range?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize that off-by-one errors happen at the boundaries. They should connect testing the start and stop conditions to checking the edges where a single change, like using < instead of <=, determines whether the loop runs one time too many or one time too few.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 2, Level 6.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Read students' AI prompts and push them to be specific: \"From what number to what number?\" \"Should it go up or down?\" \"How much should it change each time?\" Watch their tests and ask, \"Did it stop exactly where you wanted?\" to surface off-by-one errors.",
        "progression_name": "Add Counted Repetition",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to iterate: if the AI's first answer is not quite right, have them refine their prompt or manually tweak the loop rather than starting over. If students struggle with adjusting the start, stop, or step values, have them draw a number line and mark the start, each step, and the point where the stop condition becomes false."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d04a7767-1570-47d6-967b-c713dec69db3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "f926b7d3-a5ab-473b-8fbe-b8eaba586a9f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "81eb52b5-7418-4f6d-94d0-7bf0252acfec",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review each vocabulary term and its definition."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
