      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a6be9739-ce0d-4a62-a773-dccf3b97e9ca",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a6be9739-ce0d-4a62-a773-dccf3b97e9ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4f62cf77-1770-4f8d-a6fa-d08c6a298263"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "57b6ffd5-4596-4f93-9b7d-ecf9545da926",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students spot cluttered design and missing data before they start coding.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students identify the three most important fields with a partner. Then call on a few students to share with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students notice that (1) cards can feel cluttered when every field is shown, (2) some fields are clearly more important than others. Make sure students can identify truly important information rather than just the first fields they see. Ask probing questions like: Which fields would you still understand the card without? And which fields provide necessary information?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student choices of \"most important fields\" as a quick check of their initial sense of information priority."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "57b6ffd5-4596-4f93-9b7d-ecf9545da926",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd7fb77f-5e36-46e0-bbba-fbd81bc68e27"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7c7e3a9f-f89b-4e80-83fb-72eaefc55ae9",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7c7e3a9f-f89b-4e80-83fb-72eaefc55ae9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd7fb77f-5e36-46e0-bbba-fbd81bc68e27"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "69a0da55-ca67-4eb6-b6a8-e7ff55bccad0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the sample cards, Card A and Card B, which represent employees in a company directory. For each empty field in Card B, have students brainstorm a fallback idea, for example, \"Email: Unlisted\" or an idea for a placeholder image. Have students share their answers with a partner. Then call on a few students to share with the class.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Help students understand that fallbacks should make missing data clear rather than hiding it. Listen for ideas about readability, user trust, and why hiding missing info can be misleading. Ask probing questions like: If this field is missing, what would you want to see as a user? and How can hiding information lead to mistrust from users?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student fallback ideas as a quick check of their ability to handle missing data handling."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "69a0da55-ca67-4eb6-b6a8-e7ff55bccad0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd7fb77f-5e36-46e0-bbba-fbd81bc68e27"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e0667593-196d-44ba-8014-c963334e54f5",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e0667593-196d-44ba-8014-c963334e54f5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd7fb77f-5e36-46e0-bbba-fbd81bc68e27"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d531ed26-c3c4-4b8d-8d90-2e3ceb2cff7f",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What is important to consider when displaying information on cards?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should be able to explain that it is important to make cards readable by choosing and organizing appropriate fields. Websites can build user trust through honest, transparent design and by handling missing info with clear fallback choices."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d531ed26-c3c4-4b8d-8d90-2e3ceb2cff7f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd7fb77f-5e36-46e0-bbba-fbd81bc68e27"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "83c52be3-18bb-41e0-a348-21a43d783166",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Cards get messy fast. Today, we'll learn how to make them readable, handle missing info gracefully, and build trust through design.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "83c52be3-18bb-41e0-a348-21a43d783166",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd7fb77f-5e36-46e0-bbba-fbd81bc68e27"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bb306e71-dc0c-4970-a789-598cfd270dc1",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bb306e71-dc0c-4970-a789-598cfd270dc1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "dd7fb77f-5e36-46e0-bbba-fbd81bc68e27"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0e449669-3d0e-4c7d-b4ef-88147e3fadd1",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students analyze a data schema and design how its fields appear in a card layout."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0e449669-3d0e-4c7d-b4ef-88147e3fadd1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9dd08158-883f-43ab-a84a-59a68ac7a975",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Take a look at the different dataset fields like title, genre, and runtime. On the right side is a blank card. You will decide which fields from the dataset show up on the card, where they appear, and what happens if a field is missing. Use the Card Elements to design the layout of your card.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9dd08158-883f-43ab-a84a-59a68ac7a975",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "de03d621-adb5-46c5-a64f-1c96c696cd58",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 1 to complete Levels 1 through 3 or (optional) hand out the Data Schema activity guide:\n- Level 1: Students map dataset fields (title, genre, runtime, rating, imageURL, summary) onto a card design using arrows and combinations.\n- Level 2: Students read the definition of fallback value.\n- Level 3: Students explain fallback values in their own words and give an example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** In Level 1, look for designs that prioritize readability and make thoughtful use of combinations and fallbacks. For any students struggling to get started, walk them through the following steps:\n- Review the dataset fields on the schema.\n- Choose which fields to include on the card.\n- Draw arrows from each selected field to the place on the card where it should appear.\n- Allow and encourage combining or renaming fields to make them clearer (for example, merging runtime and rating into one line).",
        "progression_name": "From Schema to Layout"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "de03d621-adb5-46c5-a64f-1c96c696cd58",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ffcb13f9-a21c-49c4-950b-bf41278ecfdf",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Fallback Value",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use Level 3 responses as a formative check that students can define \"fallback value\" and provide a concrete, accurate example."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ffcb13f9-a21c-49c4-950b-bf41278ecfdf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c4656c10-4741-4b17-8330-79b365e6b556",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c4656c10-4741-4b17-8330-79b365e6b556",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ae440c30-78e7-47c5-9ba6-cc8f86f3a094",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *fallback value*.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students make a plan for fallback values.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Ask students to return to Level 1 and review their diagrams. Have them identify any fields that might be null or missing in real data (for example, no `imageURL`, no `rating`). Have them jot a clear fallback idea next to each, and also mark any fields that should be hidden entirely if missing.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students who want to hide missing data in ways that could confuse or mislead users. Prompt them with questions like: How will the user know that information is missing? and Is your fallback easy to understand?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students’ fallback notes to check that they understand the difference between \"no data\" and \"zero\" or \"none,\" and that their fallbacks maintain user trust."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students struggle to think of examples, prompt them to think of login screens, profile pictures, or reviews they have seen that show default images or \"Guest\" labels when information is missing."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ae440c30-78e7-47c5-9ba6-cc8f86f3a094",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95646771-6da7-4946-acdf-39ce5825368a",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95646771-6da7-4946-acdf-39ce5825368a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "df10ed33-0f23-48a5-8fac-fd797c67043e",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now you are going to turn your card diagrams from Sketch Lab into working code. You will code a loop to go through each object in the dataset and build a card using the fields and fallbacks you decided on.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "df10ed33-0f23-48a5-8fac-fd797c67043e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5529dcbd-cb29-468c-bebe-1ecf8545fe60",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 4 to complete Levels 4 and 5. Remind students to refer back to their diagrams on Level 1 when deciding which fields belong in the card.\n- Level 4: Students use a loop to render each record in the dataset as a card and add simple fallbacks.\n- Level 5: Students analyze three sample cards and respond to prompts about bias and transparency. Encourage students to be specific in their written responses about what feels biased or unclear, and to write a transparency label that would help a user understand how the cards are ordered or selected.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students:\n- Place field values in the intended card areas.\n- Use conditionals or checks when a value is missing or null.\n- Use fallback values for missing data. Ask: What should this card show if rating is null? and How could you make that clear to the user?",
        "progression_name": "Displaying a Schema with Cards"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5529dcbd-cb29-468c-bebe-1ecf8545fe60",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2c99aaac-a14b-4777-99e7-1d718b731680",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Free Response: Bias & Transparency Labels",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Look at students' rendered pages. Verify that multiple cards appear, fields are in consistent places, and missing data uses appropriate fallback values instead of breaking the layout. Review students' one-line labels to see if they correctly identify what is being highlighted, hidden, or sponsored, and whether they explain it in plain language."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2c99aaac-a14b-4777-99e7-1d718b731680",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0adc0a05-d871-4738-b751-b7281124adb5",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0adc0a05-d871-4738-b751-b7281124adb5",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "192fa5d4-7932-453d-805d-06ecb3629d31",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How does each version of the card influence your perception?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students should recognize the difference between the three versions:\n- Version 1: Neutral: shows a plain rating (for example, \"Rating: 2.5/5 stars\").\n- Version 2: Biased: uses hype language (for example, \"Top Value Pick!\").\n- Version 3: Hidden Info: leaves the rating off.\n\nHelp students recognize that tone, label choice (like \"Top Value Pick!\"), and hiding information (like leaving off a low rating) can bias user perception.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to connect this to real-world examples such as shopping sites, streaming services, or review platforms. Ask them, \"Where have you seen similar labeling or hiding of information in real life?\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "192fa5d4-7932-453d-805d-06ecb3629d31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d2cdeb00-0caa-411c-9373-d905951c9dd9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "85c8e567-854d-4b92-8efc-e26189ca0bac",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day. Prompt students to share brief examples from their diagrams, code, or written reflection.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Key Vocabulary."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "85c8e567-854d-4b92-8efc-e26189ca0bac",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e427d90b-06c7-411c-a838-949a0e505cd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4798275a-5ac3-4c5c-9003-c877b3926f47",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 5, Level 6 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4798275a-5ac3-4c5c-9003-c877b3926f47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e427d90b-06c7-411c-a838-949a0e505cd4"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e2b7d3e9-7079-4823-87c7-0f598bc08e94",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Activate prior knowledge about how apps filter content in students' everyday lives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-video-camera\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Video:** Show the video “Social media algorithms explained\" video.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students do not use social media, have them consider an app or website that recommends content (music, videos, shopping, or news)."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e2b7d3e9-7079-4823-87c7-0f598bc08e94",
        "lesson_activity.key": "549bc658-6ebf-4d42-9b60-cddf77b113d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "bfe1a1df-be7d-480f-809d-7a73b21ba9df",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "bfe1a1df-be7d-480f-809d-7a73b21ba9df",
        "lesson_activity.key": "549bc658-6ebf-4d42-9b60-cddf77b113d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dcbd7164-8f85-4a94-943d-f9aa03df289e",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What effect do these filters have on what you see (or don't see)? Who decides what gets filtered out?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that filters are everywhere in their digital lives, that filters both include and exclude information, and that people (designers and companies) make decisions about what gets filtered."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dcbd7164-8f85-4a94-943d-f9aa03df289e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "549bc658-6ebf-4d42-9b60-cddf77b113d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7371e5b1-6e8c-40d0-bf9b-48b515dd6b57",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we are going to look at how a simple decision by a designer can hide opportunities from users, and then we will code a filter that is transparent about what it is doing.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7371e5b1-6e8c-40d0-bf9b-48b515dd6b57",
        "lesson_activity.key": "549bc658-6ebf-4d42-9b60-cddf77b113d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e4386b52-5aa8-484d-989c-3897033ba2d4",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e4386b52-5aa8-484d-989c-3897033ba2d4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "549bc658-6ebf-4d42-9b60-cddf77b113d1"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "775bb4a9-72c4-40b3-b4be-c0336f179f92",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Help students see how hidden or default filters can exclude people even when the code is technically working.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Filtering and Transparency activity guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 6, Level 1. In their activity guides, students compare the Project Manager's \"fast and professional\" defaults to the Designer's concern that these filters exclude new grads, caregivers, and career-changers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students identifying specific groups that are excluded by the default filters and suggesting more inclusive alternatives (for example, show all job types at first, make filters very visible). Ask probing questions like: Who else might be hidden by these filters? What would a more transparent design look like?",
        "progression_name": "Inclusive and Transparent Filters",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' written responses on Level 1 to gauge whether they can connect filter settings to who is included or excluded and whether they can propose concrete changes for transparency."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "775bb4a9-72c4-40b3-b4be-c0336f179f92",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8dee2a1e-c7ab-462b-80e8-c8d673796118"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "eeaff167-ffb8-414e-a69b-1f3ea643a774",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "eeaff167-ffb8-414e-a69b-1f3ea643a774",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8dee2a1e-c7ab-462b-80e8-c8d673796118"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "67bfe4a3-76c3-498d-869f-dd688c405cb9",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Is the Job Finder app actually \"broken\"? How can code work correctly but the design still be unfair?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students distinguish between \"no bugs\" and \"good design\" and articulate that working code can still create unfair outcomes when filters hide opportunities from some groups.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *active filter*."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "67bfe4a3-76c3-498d-869f-dd688c405cb9",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8dee2a1e-c7ab-462b-80e8-c8d673796118"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4b701b20-4e14-4297-8617-8729cc6d5c47",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "This time, instead of hiding filters, we want our code and our design to be very clear about what is being filtered and why.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4b701b20-4e14-4297-8617-8729cc6d5c47",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8dee2a1e-c7ab-462b-80e8-c8d673796118"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cf2fe409-448a-4b2f-a28a-3eca003ac1d0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 2 to complete Levels 2 through 3.\n- Level 2: Students read the definition and visual explanation of \"active filter\".\n- Level 3: Students write an explanation of \"active filter\" in your own words and provide a real-world example.\n- Level 4:  Students write code to filter volunteer roles by category AND age suitability and update the on-screen text to describe the filter.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that matchesCategory correctly compares the dropdown value to each role's category and that matchesAge correctly checks whether the role is suitable for teens. Watch for students misusing && (for example, using || instead). Ask: When would this function return true? Who might be excluded if your age check is wrong?",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Active Filter"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cf2fe409-448a-4b2f-a28a-3eca003ac1d0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8dee2a1e-c7ab-462b-80e8-c8d673796118"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "72c7a833-cb32-40ac-845d-d67810ea564c",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "progression_name": "Build Filtered Volunteer Work Search ",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Quickly review the idea of AND (&&) using a simple English example on the board, such as: \"I will show this role if it is in the chosen category AND it is suitable for teens.\" Both conditions must be true."
          },
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' code on Level 4 to see whether they can correctly implement a two-part predicate with && and whether their label text accurately and clearly describes the active filters."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "72c7a833-cb32-40ac-845d-d67810ea564c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8dee2a1e-c7ab-462b-80e8-c8d673796118"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "55f694cd-6dbd-498d-97be-2f8e117d7c3f",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "55f694cd-6dbd-498d-97be-2f8e117d7c3f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8dee2a1e-c7ab-462b-80e8-c8d673796118"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1b6900b1-a069-4899-b26f-786cdfb623fe",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Define *filter bubble*.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How could the active filters you coded in the Volunteer Finder create a filter bubble if users do not know they are there?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect their specific code (active filters and && logic) to the broader idea that hidden filters can limit what users see, reducing diverse options or perspectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 6, Level 5 to complete Levels 5 through 6.\n- Level 5: Read the definition and visual for \"Filter Bubble\".\n- Level 6: Explain filter bubbles in your own words and give an example, ideally linked to the Volunteer Finder or another app.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who understand that personalized or hidden filters can narrow what users see over time. Ask: If someone always chooses the same category, what opportunities might they miss? How could more transparent filters help avoid a filter bubble?",
        "progression_name": "Vocab: Filter Bubble",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Invite a few volunteers to share their examples aloud to surface different ways filter bubbles can appear in social media, shopping, or recommendation apps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1b6900b1-a069-4899-b26f-786cdfb623fe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "8dee2a1e-c7ab-462b-80e8-c8d673796118"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "78dd42e3-58fa-42e6-8d25-0f8920fe7c31",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review each vocabulary term and definition, asking students for a quick example for each from today's activities."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "78dd42e3-58fa-42e6-8d25-0f8920fe7c31",
        "lesson_activity.key": "419ac5f4-c196-4ec5-9760-d91d6419a3f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e72ba260-f2df-4165-965f-ae26439f3408",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 1, Level 6 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e72ba260-f2df-4165-965f-ae26439f3408",
        "lesson_activity.key": "419ac5f4-c196-4ec5-9760-d91d6419a3f3"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "cdf45dc1-b37e-4e2b-9054-ab7daec627da",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Students will physically represent an array so they can see what happens when they change originals versus copies of the original.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Pure Functions vs Side Effects activity guide and a small stack of sticky notes.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the \"Sticky Note Playlist\" warm-up.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how students should shuffle their playlist.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Is there a way to go back to your original song order? Why or why not?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Help students notice that once they mutate the original arrangement, they cannot easily recover the exact original without having saved it somewhere or recreating it entirely."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "cdf45dc1-b37e-4e2b-9054-ab7daec627da",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ec3e513-1fb9-44c3-a50b-8f07f47061ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a3c608cb-051e-4432-a2b2-d197bcab2d3d",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You just saw how easy it is to \"lose\" your original data. When we code, this is called \"mutation\" or a \"side effect.\" Now let’s explore how we can avoid having this happen.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a3c608cb-051e-4432-a2b2-d197bcab2d3d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ec3e513-1fb9-44c3-a50b-8f07f47061ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "867ea75a-559e-4471-b584-90a8dfb6b84f",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Tell students to return their sticky notes to their initial order in the Original Array. Have them create a second copy of each song on a new post-it and shuffle only the new set into a different order in the Updated Array.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Click through the animation to demonstrate how students should make a copy and then shuffle their new playlist.\n\n<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Connect everyday experiences with photos to the importance of preserving originals in software.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *How is this shuffle different from the first one? What stayed the same and what changed?*\n- *If you want to edit a photo, what advantages do you get by making a copy of the photo and only editing the copy?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students articulate that the first approach mutated or destroyed the original data, while the second approach kept the original intact and only changed a copy of the data. Help students understand how it is possible to recover from mistakes when the original is preserved. Give the class a shared starting point for understanding why copying or preserving originals matters in software design and how this builds user trust."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "867ea75a-559e-4471-b584-90a8dfb6b84f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ec3e513-1fb9-44c3-a50b-8f07f47061ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "def8abc9-dae0-406c-a927-386af27c8ef2",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Today we'll explore how these same ideas appear in code. Just like photo apps protect your originals, programmers use pure functions to transform data safely—without breaking what came before.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "def8abc9-dae0-406c-a927-386af27c8ef2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ec3e513-1fb9-44c3-a50b-8f07f47061ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "48c8e6f6-5eea-4cfb-9198-4cf53a3088ef",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "48c8e6f6-5eea-4cfb-9198-4cf53a3088ef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "1ec3e513-1fb9-44c3-a50b-8f07f47061ef"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d9e09c76-bf85-4e3e-a4e4-a664b595b559",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 7, Level 1 to complete the Method Investigator section of their activity guide.\n- Level 1: Investigate Pure Methods and Mutating Methods\n- Level 2: Read the definition of \"pure function.\"\n- Level 3: Write a short explanation in their own words and include an example.\n- Level 4: Read the definition of \"side effect.\"\n- Level 5: Write a short explanation in their own words and include an example.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students who are only clicking buttons without comparing the two arrays. Prompt them to describe out loud what changed, if anything, in the original array. Ask questions like: What stayed the same? What is different? What happens if you click the same method button again?",
        "progression_name": "Comparing Methods"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d9e09c76-bf85-4e3e-a4e4-a664b595b559",
        "lesson_activity.key": "510961ea-5edc-4cc7-965e-88284ec59926"
      }
    },
