      }
    },
    {
      "key": "19d4073e-274d-4d3f-a124-3f077cf38d15",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 9, Level 8 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "19d4073e-274d-4d3f-a124-3f077cf38d15",
        "lesson_activity.key": "193c2d4c-e32b-45a1-a15e-278a6f8571f9"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "95e83bef-62b0-475a-899c-895dfb96b083",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Engage students with how vague field names can lead to very different interpretations.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Display the form example and briefly introduce that two people and an AI disagree about what some fields mean.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click through the animated slide to display the prompts.\n- *Which field names caused the biggest misunderstanding? Why?*\n- *How can small wording choices shape how AI interprets meaning?*\n- *Why might designing clear schemas be an ethical responsibility when AI uses your data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students recognize that designing clear, unambiguous schemas is an ethical responsibility, as vague fields and unclear types cause misinterpretation, not that any person or AI is inherently wrong.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the role of a Database Architect role."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "95e83bef-62b0-475a-899c-895dfb96b083",
        "lesson_activity.key": "69184c8f-a94a-4874-ae10-eb5f1791362e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0546e43f-e752-42c3-b346-6e7fce193da1",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "As we work today, you will act like database architects, making choices about field names and data types.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0546e43f-e752-42c3-b346-6e7fce193da1",
        "lesson_activity.key": "69184c8f-a94a-4874-ae10-eb5f1791362e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9ad23068-e05d-4928-9f12-042b8471a858",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Introduce the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9ad23068-e05d-4928-9f12-042b8471a858",
        "lesson_activity.key": "69184c8f-a94a-4874-ae10-eb5f1791362e"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "899b4d24-b4e7-40a4-883d-5e61a6d0e374",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-bullseye-arrow\"></i> **Goal of this activity:** Have students investigate how unclear field names and types confuse AI and begin revising a schema to improve reasoning."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "899b4d24-b4e7-40a4-883d-5e61a6d0e374",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fdeb59fc-2aa5-4064-8125-c2f7b5ed2d61",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Imagine the AI is doing the best it can, but our data labels are sending mixed messages. We will debug the data, not just the code.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fdeb59fc-2aa5-4064-8125-c2f7b5ed2d61",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3d45a55d-a1e4-4501-af6e-fa649edd15bf",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 10, Level 1. Have students review the data and use AI to spot inconsistent field names and data types.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for students identifying issues like vague labels (\"stars\" vs \"rating\"), mixed types (\"3\" as a string vs 3 as a number), or missing values. Ask probing questions like: What do you want this field to mean? How could you rename it to make that obvious to both a person and an AI? Encourage students to test their changes by rerunning the AI check.",
        "progression_name": "Investigate Misleading Fields",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If students get stuck, suggest they write one clear sentence for what a field is supposed to represent, then turn that sentence into a better field name and data type."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3d45a55d-a1e4-4501-af6e-fa649edd15bf",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dda6816b-cfcd-44c6-a20d-8ebf5b852604",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dda6816b-cfcd-44c6-a20d-8ebf5b852604",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e7d5dc9e-84e4-47c5-a16c-15651738755e",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *How did changing field names or types change what the AI concluded about the data?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students connect specific schema changes (like renaming \"stars\" to \"rating_1_to_5\" or changing a string \"3\" to a number 3) to changes in AI behavior, seeing that clearer schemas lead to more accurate reasoning."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e7d5dc9e-84e4-47c5-a16c-15651738755e",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2251b555-49f5-454e-a4f0-0790759d6b93",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "Now that you have seen how a messy schema confuses the AI, you will build and clean the data foundation for a new app.",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2251b555-49f5-454e-a4f0-0790759d6b93",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5e499c11-44e7-4f67-b60d-0b6b04de2061",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to complete Lesson 10, Level 2. Have students design and clean a schema so the app's features work reliably.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students write loops that touch every object and actually modify keys and values. Ask: What fields will every project need? What should the type of this field be? How are you handling missing or extreme values? Support students in testing edge cases like empty lists or unusual values.",
        "progression_name": "Data Architect Challenge",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage pairs to talk through one example object out loud—what it looks like before and after cleaning—before they generalize to a loop."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5e499c11-44e7-4f67-b60d-0b6b04de2061",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "dd42ebf4-1cae-46ed-a0d6-eceb79101a2f",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "dd42ebf4-1cae-46ed-a0d6-eceb79101a2f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5f2f2002-99ad-4578-a66b-45d45f51983c",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *If you were a QA tester, what edge cases would you test to make sure the schema really works?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students see that QA testers and data architects work together: QA uses edge cases to find weaknesses in the schema and constraints, and good schemas make apps more robust and fair.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 2 to extend their Level 2 work by adding constraint checks and running edge-case tests.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for clear conditionals that enforce constraints and for students who actually try unusual inputs. Ask: What happens if there are no projects? What if a project is missing a grade? How would your AI teammate react to these cases?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use student explanations of which constraints they chose and why as evidence of understanding how schemas, constraints, and edge cases connect."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5f2f2002-99ad-4578-a66b-45d45f51983c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "f95bda43-3c39-41f7-9ff1-b7a75ad4bb29",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students turn and talk for one minute to connect a concrete schema decision to the Question of the Day.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Listen for students who mention specific field names, data types, or constraints. Nudge them to link their decisions to user experience or fairness (for example, not mislabeling projects or excluding edge cases).\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 3 to complete a short written reflection.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Look for explanations that mention both the original schema problem and the updated field name or type. Prompt students to be precise: What exactly did the AI assume before? What does it assume now?",
        "progression_name": "Reflection: Justify Your Design Choice",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "Use students' written justifications as a key formative check that they understand how schema decisions affect AI reasoning."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "f95bda43-3c39-41f7-9ff1-b7a75ad4bb29",
        "lesson_activity.key": "5b1298fe-ade4-4027-9a4a-fb807d17d58b"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fd52e618-573d-4335-b200-8341b6eb04c2",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the lesson objectives.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the Question of the Day."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fd52e618-573d-4335-b200-8341b6eb04c2",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e648978-19fa-4351-af87-4296678aad34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "7f74335a-e789-45a7-90e2-fd421c344f09",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Lesson 10, Level 4 to complete the End of Lesson Survey.",
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
        "progression_name": "End of Lesson Survey"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "7f74335a-e789-45a7-90e2-fd421c344f09",
        "lesson_activity.key": "7e648978-19fa-4351-af87-4296678aad34"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "4dd97114-7eea-4855-a6d9-297139d434ca",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts.\n- *When you're trying to find information in an app or website, what makes it easy or hard to explore the data?*\n- *What is one thing you wish more apps did to help you find what you're looking for?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on real experiences with findability, data clarity, and user control, setting up the project's focus on schema design, transparency, and meaningful filtering.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "4dd97114-7eea-4855-a6d9-297139d434ca",
        "lesson_activity.key": "538eb1c3-b000-4273-8305-34a406a8ed39"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "5faaeb45-73c9-4d9a-aae4-4853f522ffa4",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Give each student a copy of the Interactive Data Dashboard Project Planning Guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Walk students through these simplified project goals. These summarize what their dashboard must do: display data clearly, support user control through filters and sorting, and communicate choices transparently. Emphasize that fairness and visibility—like which items get surfaced or hidden—will be part of their design thinking throughout the project.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to read the rubric on the last two pages of the project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What do you notice about the project requirements and rubric? What does success look like for this project?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students share what they notice about the project requirements and rubric and identify the components to implement in their program. Listen for students noticing themes like transparency, fairness, active filter displays, loop-driven rendering, and Reset/Undo—these are central to the project and will guide their design decisions.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the milestone structure of the project. Explain that the Interactive Data Dashboard Project is divided into four milestones, each with its own goals and deliverables. Use the visual roadmap to preview the journey students will take — from choosing and refining their dataset to building and showcasing their interactive dashboard.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that milestones help them manage time and track progress, just like in real design and development projects. Encourage them to think of each milestone as a \"checkpoint\" to reflect on what's working, what needs improvement, and how their design aligns with their user and purpose."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "5faaeb45-73c9-4d9a-aae4-4853f522ffa4",
        "lesson_activity.key": "538eb1c3-b000-4273-8305-34a406a8ed39"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1d8c50da-43e3-4a5e-833f-6481487ea251",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1d8c50da-43e3-4a5e-833f-6481487ea251",
        "lesson_activity.key": "538eb1c3-b000-4273-8305-34a406a8ed39"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68288898-4e8f-4552-b73c-cc900f2a7017",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Explain that today's focus is Milestone 1: Choose a Dataset & Define Your Schema, where students select a dataset, narrow it to meaningful fields, and examine it for bias, clarity, and findability. Direct students to open their Interactive Data Dashboard Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 section. Have them read through the Goals and Deliverables so they understand what needs to be completed by the end of this milestone.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to highlight or note parts of the deliverables that feel most exciting or challenging. This helps you gauge engagement and plan support for different groups. Remind students that Milestone 1 focuses on data decisions — not code. Their goal is to choose a dataset, define a schema, and think about visibility, fairness, and which fields users will filter by."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68288898-4e8f-4552-b73c-cc900f2a7017",
        "lesson_activity.key": "538eb1c3-b000-4273-8305-34a406a8ed39"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "03042419-86fb-418c-9e7e-aff133fd3bc0",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "03042419-86fb-418c-9e7e-aff133fd3bc0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "538eb1c3-b000-4273-8305-34a406a8ed39"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "e23f1a67-2e24-461b-aeba-f5646d621b5a",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct student to Level 1 to begin Milestone 1: Choose a Dataset & Define Your Schema. Students can choose the tool that best supports their current task: exploring datasets, organizing their plan, identifying filter-relevant fields, sketching their schema, or reviewing their data choices for visibility and fairness.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students explore the hub, ask guiding questions such as:\n- “Which dataset does your project focus on?”\n- “What fields will be included in your filters and why?”\n- “Are there any missing groups or unclear categories in your filters or labels?”\n- “Who might not be visible due to your filter choices right now?”\n\nUse these check-ins to confirm students are moving from idea to planning, rather than jumping straight into code.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone One",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Encourage students to experiment with multiple tools as they develop their plan — for example, brainstorming with the Filter and Threshold Planning Bot before designing their schema. Remind them that testing and refining their ideas here will save time later once they start coding."
          },
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Set expectations for pacing — Milestone 1 can typically be finished by the end of today's class or early next class. Encourage students to focus first on clarity of idea rather than polish."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "e23f1a67-2e24-461b-aeba-f5646d621b5a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "538eb1c3-b000-4273-8305-34a406a8ed39"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "b74f0ed1-8837-40d0-9a0e-28f0e570c854",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "b74f0ed1-8837-40d0-9a0e-28f0e570c854",
        "lesson_activity.key": "538eb1c3-b000-4273-8305-34a406a8ed39"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "730646f5-7ce3-4955-b130-960dad32c04a",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 1 Reflection section in their project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are discussing and sharing these reflection questions with a thought partner.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few volunteers to share aloud or conduct a quick pair-and-share to close."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "730646f5-7ce3-4955-b130-960dad32c04a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "538eb1c3-b000-4273-8305-34a406a8ed39"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "fc89be17-74f9-4e84-a3a2-4161a34401fd",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** Click the animated slide to display the discussion prompts:\n- *When you look at a page full of information (menus, playlists, search results, etc.), what helps you understand it quickly?*\n- *What kinds of layouts or labels make information easier—or harder—to scan?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students surface real-world examples of clear labeling, visual grouping, and layout logic, preparing them for dashboard wireframing and schema → UI decisions.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "fc89be17-74f9-4e84-a3a2-4161a34401fd",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c961247-9fba-49a2-9ccc-b3367283cd1c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "9aaae265-aede-428f-ae2c-be9d562cc300",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 2 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on completing Milestone 1 and starting work on Milestone 2. Direct students to open their Interactive Data Dashboard Project Guide and locate the Milestone 1 and 2 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to use Level 1 if they still need to complete any Milestone 1 work or would benefit from one of the support tools, and move to Level 2 to begin Milestone 2, where they will shift from exploring and refining a dataset and outlining their schema, to planning and building their dashboard. Explain that today’s focus is creating clear wireframes and building their static UI that displays all items using loop-driven rendering. Emphasize that students should aim for clarity rather than polish—simple layouts that show where cards will go and how each schema field will be labeled. Encourage students to begin with one clean card layout and build from there, mirroring how developers prototype simple, testable pieces before adding complexity.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task and progressing toward a complete first draft. Ask check-in questions like:\n- Which schema fields will appear on your card and where will they go?\n- Where will users look first when they open your dashboard?\n- Does your wireframe show clear labels for each field and for any future filters?\n- How will your layout make the data easy to scan and compare (e.g., grouping, order, or emphasis)?\n- Where will transparency cues like “Showing X results” or active filter labels appear?\n\nRemind students that their wireframes should clearly map schema fields to card UI elements and show where labels and transparency cues (like “Showing X results”) will go. Provide quick support if students get stuck — redirect them to the Filter & Threshold Planner Bot or Project Support Bot for troubleshooting help before intervening directly.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone Two"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "9aaae265-aede-428f-ae2c-be9d562cc300",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c961247-9fba-49a2-9ccc-b3367283cd1c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "786e8603-983a-4e53-87a0-2f769093089c",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 2 Reflection section in their project planning guide.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write and discuss, listen for reflections that describe how their page layout and card design help (or fail to help) users scan, compare, and find items quickly and clearly. Encourage students to notice whether their labels, headings, and visual structure make the dataset easy to scan, and to capture specific ideas about what they might refine next time.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time permits, invite a few students to share one design choice that makes their data easier to understand. Highlight that noticing how layout supports clarity is an important design skill — it shows they’re thinking about how real users will read and interpret the information in their app."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "786e8603-983a-4e53-87a0-2f769093089c",
        "lesson_activity.key": "4c961247-9fba-49a2-9ccc-b3367283cd1c"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "58d522ea-cc73-4fe5-a668-db27e17ffbd0",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *Looking at your dashboard so far, what's one thing you want users to be able to find easily — and what’s one thing that might still be hard to find?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students reflect on how their current dashboard layout supports or challenges visibility and findability. This prepares them to design filters, sorting, and transparency features in ways that help users explore the dataset meaningfully.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "58d522ea-cc73-4fe5-a668-db27e17ffbd0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0c114b4-88b5-40f4-b194-a6104e968769"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c3128d13-53ed-44f8-aea3-2df902f58aef",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 3 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 2 and 3. Direct students to open their Interactive Data Dashboard Project Guide and locate the Milestone 2 and 3 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Point out that these milestones move from shaping how data is displayed to making that data interactive. Encourage students not to aim for perfection at first — focus on getting one feature or transformation working clearly, and then improve it through testing, debugging, and fairness checks."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c3128d13-53ed-44f8-aea3-2df902f58aef",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0c114b4-88b5-40f4-b194-a6104e968769"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2613910a-e177-4f5c-b27d-50aec2c842f0",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2613910a-e177-4f5c-b27d-50aec2c842f0",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0c114b4-88b5-40f4-b194-a6104e968769"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "22215e2a-6c02-472d-9329-f0850be59cfe",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3. Encourage students to TAG areas like filter accuracy, sort behavior, active label updates, and Reset/Undo function. This helps them gather early insights before debugging more deeply.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\n\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "22215e2a-6c02-472d-9329-f0850be59cfe",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0c114b4-88b5-40f4-b194-a6104e968769"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ba08ba45-c3a3-486c-9fd2-d538c22e2978",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ba08ba45-c3a3-486c-9fd2-d538c22e2978",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0c114b4-88b5-40f4-b194-a6104e968769"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "8c3982df-0643-49a7-aff9-cd3bc01884fc",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Milestone 3 Reflection section in their project planning guide. Let students know they can continue to add on to this reflection as they continue to make progress on Milestone 3 and conduct more testing.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "If time allows, invite a few students to share how their filter or sort choices influence which items appear first — this helps reinforce fairness and visibility thinking from the rubric. Highlight that catching fairness issues or algorithmic quirks early is a sign of strong computational thinking — developers constantly refine how data is displayed to ensure experiences are both accurate and equitable."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "8c3982df-0643-49a7-aff9-cd3bc01884fc",
        "lesson_activity.key": "e0c114b4-88b5-40f4-b194-a6104e968769"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "13414bd8-7a54-45de-8db1-2e23a49d7f44",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one part of your dashboard that you think might break or behave unexpectedly if someone tried an unusual filter or sorting choice? Why that part?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students anticipate potential weaknesses or edge cases in their current filtering and sorting logic, preparing them for systematic debugging and refinement in Milestone 4.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "13414bd8-7a54-45de-8db1-2e23a49d7f44",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d1943221-bc97-43e9-a599-5f8e994656ed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c01796f5-e8c5-4d54-b8bf-4bcf0c418b03",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 4 of the project. Explain that today students will focus on Milestones 3 and 4. Direct students to open their Interactive Data Dashboard Project Guide and locate the Milestone 3 and 4 sections. Have them review the Goals and Deliverables for each milestone so they understand what to prioritize during work time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students pair up or form small groups and TAG one to two peers' projects. They can leave feedback on sticky notes (digital or paper) directly at their peer's workspace or comment within the project guide if using shared docs. Emphasize that this is not a final critique — it's a chance to gather early insights as part of Milestone 3. Encourage students to TAG areas like: filter accuracy, sort behavior, active filter labels, Rest/Undo, or fairness issues.",
        "duration": "35",
        "name": "Activity",
        "progression_name": "Milestone Three",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Students may be at different points when it's time for peer review. Reassure them that work doesn't need to be complete to be reviewed — feedback during the process helps improve the final product.\nReinforce the mindset that design and development are iterative, and peer feedback offers new perspectives and guidance.\n\nIf time allows, invite a few students to share one piece of feedback they received that will guide their next steps."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c01796f5-e8c5-4d54-b8bf-4bcf0c418b03",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d1943221-bc97-43e9-a599-5f8e994656ed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "a1a67aab-7168-44d9-ab4b-b23f4757248d",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "a1a67aab-7168-44d9-ab4b-b23f4757248d",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d1943221-bc97-43e9-a599-5f8e994656ed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "d290a95e-a699-4135-99c4-9d9263281ecb",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to revisit the Milestone 3 Reflection section in their project planning guide and add on to what they wrote the previous class period. They should update their Fixes + Features List, record any new debugging insights, and reflect on how their filter and sort choices influence which items users see first.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "When students revisit their reflections today, point out that adding new insights after another round of testing is exactly how professional engineers document progress. Encourage them to notice what changed, what improved, and what new questions emerged—this shows they’re thinking like real developers who learn from every test cycle to make smarter, more informed decisions."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "d290a95e-a699-4135-99c4-9d9263281ecb",
        "lesson_activity.key": "d1943221-bc97-43e9-a599-5f8e994656ed"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "1e661b54-59c6-44c1-a6c0-16a9119084a3",
      "position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-comments\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discuss:** *What's one edge case, label, or detail you want to check today to make sure your dashboard is clear, fair, and ready to share?*\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-lightbulb-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Discussion Goal:** Students identify final improvement opportunities tied to clarity, transparency, fairness, or reliability, setting a concrete intention for their last work session.",
        "duration": "5",
        "name": "Warm Up"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "1e661b54-59c6-44c1-a6c0-16a9119084a3",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ebd9e881-f28c-4e04-9032-7d9ef64a842f",
      "position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Review the tasks to complete for Day 5 of the project. Explain that today is focused on Milestone 4: Handle Edge Cases & Finalize Your Dashboard, where students test their dashboard with tricky cases, polish their UI and logic, refine for clarity and inclusivity, and make it ready for showcasing. Remind students to check their Interactive Data Dashboard Project Guide for the Milestone 4 deliverables.",
        "duration": "30",
        "name": "Activity",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Clarify that students should test unusual filter combinations, missing values, empty result states, and sorting after filtering. These edge cases help ensure the dashboard behaves predictably, transparently, and fairly. Encourage students to check that transparency elements — such as “Showing X results” and active filter labels — always match the state of the data. Remind students that even small adjustments to labels, spacing, or ordering can make a big difference for user clarity."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "ebd9e881-f28c-4e04-9032-7d9ef64a842f",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c93e726b-f79a-416a-98c7-451400e7e37a",
      "position": 3,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c93e726b-f79a-416a-98c7-451400e7e37a",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0811109d-b9e3-40c0-abb6-824156c8b497",
      "position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to the last two pages of their Interactive Data Dashboard Project Guide, where the project rubric is located. Have students review each category carefully and mark where they believe their project currently falls. Encourage honesty — the goal is to identify strengths and areas for continued growth as intentional designers.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Check that students are engaging thoughtfully with each rubric criterion, not rushing to completion. Encourage students to examine the Ethical Reasoning: Transparency & Fairness row carefully — this project’s dashboard focuses heavily on ensuring filters do not unintentionally hide important items or groups. Under Algorithmic Features, remind students to check whether their Reset/Undo is reliable and whether active filter counts update correctly.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that self-assessment isn't just about scoring their project — it's a professional habit that helps them take ownership of their design choices. Say something like: \"Designers constantly evaluate their own work to make sure it aligns with their goals, audience, and purpose. This rubric helps you see where you've grown and what you can still refine.\"\n\nIf time allows, have students highlight one category they feel especially proud of and one they'd like to improve next time."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0811109d-b9e3-40c0-abb6-824156c8b497",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "68a649c7-0555-4100-973d-83e3fb92bd36",
      "position": 5,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "68a649c7-0555-4100-973d-83e3fb92bd36",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "2e3c82b7-513e-4820-be0c-1fec33bd9460",
      "position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-desktop\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Transition:** Direct students to Level 4 to finish developing their Interactive Data Dashboard Projects. Explain that this is their final dedicated work session for Milestone 4: Handle Edge Cases & Finalize Your Dashboard. Reinforce that today's goal is to prioritize improvements based on their self-assessment and TAG peer feedback, rather than trying to fix everything at once. Instruct students to use the feedback they received from their TAG review and self-assessment to finalize their Interactive Data Dashboard Project. Have students submit their projects at the end of the allotted time.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Ensure students are on task. Remind students to comment their code with meaningful descriptions. Have students review their rubric and prioritize work on any rows where they do not yet show extensive evidence. Check that students are recording changes in their Milestone 4 Evidence + Iteration Log. Their documentation should show how they tested edge cases, revised filters/labels/thresholds, refined UI clarity, and used AI in their debugging.",
        "progression_name": "Milestone Four",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Remind students that professional designers always prioritize fixes before a release. Say something like: \"You don't need to fix everything today — focus on what will have the biggest impact for your user. Iteration is about making smart choices with the time you have.\"\n\nIf time allows at the end, invite students to do a brief pair preview, where they open a peer's site and give one final piece of positive or constructive feedback."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "2e3c82b7-513e-4820-be0c-1fec33bd9460",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3de6d1ca-d045-4c52-8b07-7ad1c394ed49",
      "position": 7,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3de6d1ca-d045-4c52-8b07-7ad1c394ed49",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "3975c4bc-c331-4ce9-a078-08d84a20b788",
      "position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-file-text-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Distribute:** Distribute several sticky notes to each student for the gallery walk.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Have students begin the Gallery Walk by displaying their Interactive Data Dashboard Apps on their computers, then moving around the room to explore classmates' projects. As they visit each one, students should use their sticky notes to leave feedback, kudos, or questions following the TAG approach (Tell, Ask, Give). Encourage them to engage with a range of projects—not just those of close friends.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** Move around the room to observe how students are engaging with the displayed projects. Encourage feedback on clarity, fairness, and transparency. For example:\n- Is it clear what filters are active?\n- Are any items consistently hidden or hard to surface?\n- Does Reset/Undo behave predictably?\n- Is the dataset easy to scan visually?\n- Do labels accurately reflect the data shown?",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "teachingTip",
            "markdown": "Frame the Gallery Walk as a celebration of learning and creativity, not a critique. Say something like: \"This is your chance to see how everyone's ideas came to life in different ways. You might even get inspired by someone else's approach!\""
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "3975c4bc-c331-4ce9-a078-08d84a20b788",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "c2283f97-3f01-4b86-abc2-e44e01345876",
      "position": 9,
      "properties": {
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "c2283f97-3f01-4b86-abc2-e44e01345876",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "0950f734-6f02-4a7a-b5ef-f68b3b2c13b8",
      "position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "description": "You've gone from choosing a dataset and defining a schema to building, testing, and refining your Interactive Data Dashboard. This final reflection is a chance to capture how your dashboard evolved — how your filtering and sorting logic improved, how you handled edge cases, and how your design supports fairness, clarity, and user control. It also gives you space to reflect on how you used AI throughout the project, including when it helped you think through logic, debug issues, or refine your code.",
        "duration": "10",
        "name": "Wrap Up",
        "remarks": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "0950f734-6f02-4a7a-b5ef-f68b3b2c13b8",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "863e409a-715b-42e8-bf13-b4b9549b6181",
      "position": 11,
      "properties": {
        "description": "<i class=\"fa fa-check-square-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Do This:** Direct students to complete the Final Reflection and Final Self-Check sections in their Interactive Data Dashboard Project Guide. Explain that this final step helps them consolidate what they learned about: designing clear, user-friendly dashboards, making filtering and sorting transparent and fair, handling edge cases and improving reliability, and using AI intentionally and evaluating its impact. Emphasize that their answers should include specific examples from their own dashboards.\n\n<i class=\"fa fa-refresh\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i> **Circulate:** As students write, encourage them to reference concrete moments from their project:\n- an edge case that revealed a bug\n- a filter or sorting choice they revised for fairness or clarity\n- how transparency cues (“Showing X results,” active filter labels) improved user control\n- how they evaluated, modified, or rejected AI suggestions\n\nProbe with questions like:\n- “What changed in your dashboard between Milestone 1 and Milestone 4?”\n- “How did your Reset/Undo or active filter labels improve over time?”\n- “How did AI help you understand or debug your logic?”\n\nIf students finish early, prompt them to help a peer reflect or revisit the rubric one more time.",
        "tips": [
          {
            "type": "assessmentOpportunity",
            "markdown": "- You can use the Interactive Data Dashboard Planning Guide, Milestone evidence, and Final Reflection as assessment for the Process, Iteration, and Code/UI Quality rubric row.\n- The Final Reflection also provides evidence for Ethical Reasoning: Transparency & Fairness, since students explain how their filtering and sorting choices affected visibility and user control.\n- Code excerpts and screenshots from Milestones 2–4 can serve as evidence for Schema & Dataset Design, Dynamic Rendering with Loops, and Algorithmic Features, depending on what students choose to showcase and annotate."
          }
        ]
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "activity_section.key": "863e409a-715b-42e8-bf13-b4b9549b6181",
        "lesson_activity.key": "257c8006-ce44-40e3-9672-2c6e9d7f668f"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "ce2b32f4-ec69-4fec-b506-1e75da22ea34",
      "position": 12,
      "properties": {
        "name": "End of Lesson Survey",
